put(groups[0],'Problem_Statement.md','''# Insurance Document Intelligence for Automated Policy and Claim Intake\n\n**Riki Banik | Roll 33 | HSR-AIML-004**\n\n## Problem\nScanned policy and claim intake pages may contain uneven illumination, noise, skew, and varied layouts. Reviewers may need to locate identifiers and compare repeated values across documents. This project evaluates a local academic prototype for image preparation, OCR, candidate field extraction, and cross-document consistency review.\n\n## Objectives\n- Load PDFs and images and preserve page-level outputs.\n- Compare independent OpenCV preprocessing variants with Tesseract.\n- Classify likely document type and extract candidate fields with evidence.\n- Flag inconsistencies for human review; do not decide coverage or eligibility.\n- Audit metrics, references, and limitations transparently.\n\nThe small synthetic sample is not representative real-world insurance data. The prototype is not production-ready or deployed by an insurer.''')
put(groups[1],'State_of_the_Art.md','''# State of the Art\n\nDocument OCR combines image preparation, text recognition, and semantic extraction. Global thresholding uses a common intensity cutoff; Otsu selects a threshold from gray-level histogram class separation [1]. CLAHE improves local contrast with clipped histogram amplification [2]. Morphology changes foreground structures according to a structuring element, which can remove faint strokes or join glyphs. These operations have image-dependent effects.\n\nTesseract offers page segmentation modes that encode layout assumptions [3]. PSM 3 performs automatic segmentation; PSM 6 assumes one uniform text block; PSM 11 targets sparse text. CER and WER measure edit distance normalized by reference characters or words, and depend on transcription fidelity, tokenization, normalization, and order. FUNSD is a noisy scanned form dataset [4], used separately here as a general-form benchmark rather than insurance evidence.\n\nThis work integrates established operations into an inspectable local workflow. Its contribution is engineering integration and descriptive comparison, not a new OCR or image-processing algorithm. Results on a small synthetic sample are exploratory.\n\n## References\n[1] N. Otsu, “A Threshold Selection Method from Gray-Level Histograms,” IEEE Trans. Syst., Man, Cybern., vol. 9, no. 1, pp. 62–66, 1979, doi: 10.1109/TSMC.1979.4310076.\n[2] K. Zuiderveld, “Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization,” in Graphics Gems IV, 1994, pp. 474–485.\n[3] R. Smith, “An Overview of the Tesseract OCR Engine,” Proc. ICDAR, 2007, pp. 629–633, doi: 10.1109/ICDAR.2007.4376991.\n[4] G. Jaume et al., “FUNSD: A Dataset for Form Understanding in Noisy Scanned Documents,” Proc. ICDAR Workshops, 2019, doi: 10.1109/ICDARW.2019.10029. Verify full author metadata and pagination against publisher record before submission.''')
put(groups[2],'Methodology_and_Pseudocode.md','''# Methodology, Flowchart, and Pseudocode\n\n**Workflow:** PDF/image → validation and rasterization → independent OpenCV variants → Tesseract OCR per variant/PSM → classification and evidence-linked field extraction → cross-document consistency rules → Streamlit human review.\n\n`process_document()` is the shared orchestration. `build_variants()` provides original, grayscale, CLAHE, histogram equalization, global/Otsu/adaptive thresholds, Gaussian/median denoising, morphology, and deskew. The insurance OCR experiment covers 23 eligible scans, three PSMs, and 19 paired PDF-text references; four unmatched scans are not scored. Scores remain provisional because reference text was not exhaustively aligned to scan pixels. FUNSD test evaluation is separate; its training split is not used.\n\n```text\nfor document in inputs:\n  pages = load_and_rasterize(document)\n  for page in pages:\n    views = build_variants(page)\n    for view in views:\n      for psm in configured_psms:\n        result = tesseract(view, psm)\n        save_text_status_confidence_and_parameters(result)\n    choose_confidence_ranked_view_for_application_extraction()\n    classify_and_extract_fields_with_evidence()\npersist_json_and_sqlite_records()\ncompare_related_document_fields()\nrender_review_views_in_streamlit()\n```\n\nEvaluation scores every saved view/PSM independently; confidence-selected application output does not replace per-variant experimental predictions.''')
put(groups[4],'Dataset_and_Data_Sources.md','''# Dataset and Data Sources\n\nThe local inventory includes 88 Strata manifest records (synthetic) and 10 Gritworks PDFs. Twenty-three eligible scans were processed, 19 have same-stem PDF text references, and four police-report scans have no paired PDF. Gritworks is inventory-only. The sample is small, synthetic, and not representative of real-world insurance.\n\nFUNSD is stored separately at `datasets/external/funsd/`. Its 50 testing images are used in the supplementary form OCR study; 149 training pages are not used for that evaluation. FUNSD is a general form dataset, not insurance data.\n\nOriginal datasets remain in place and are not duplicated into this submission package. See project dataset notes for provenance and licensing. External datasets may require separate instructor-approved access.''')
put(groups[5],'Novelty_and_USP.md','''# Contribution and Utility\n\nThis prototype brings image enhancement alternatives, per-view OCR, evidence-bearing field extraction, cross-document consistency flags, and human review into one local workflow. Intermediate views and values remain inspectable.\n\nThe contribution is integration and a descriptive experiment using established methods. It does not claim a new OCR model or algorithm, representative real-world insurance accuracy, automated claim approval, statistical significance, or insurer deployment.''')
put(groups[6],'Results_Interpretation_and_Limitations.md','''# Results: Verified Calculations and Provisional Comparisons\n\nThe independent audit recalculated 627/627 scored CER/WER entries from saved predictions and PDF-derived references. Each PSM/variant used 19 paired documents; four unpaired scans were unscored. No duplicate evaluation keys or empty scored references were found. This verifies arithmetic and record integrity, not reference fidelity.\n\nMacro CER/WER, Original → CLAHE: PSM 3, 0.306785/0.377461 → 0.177384/0.271460; PSM 6, 0.329215/0.421155 → 0.224573/0.328093; PSM 11, 0.287972/0.377817 → 0.142398/0.248395. Comparisons are provisional because PDF text was not exhaustively aligned to scans. No statistical test was performed.\n\nPSM 3 Original metadata evaluation had 100/124 exact matches; 24 unmatched values were classified as 3 incorrect, 12 missing, 9 reference ambiguity, 0 unavailable.\n\nThe sample is synthetic and small, entities recur, and no held-out split exists. Reference order affects scores. Four scans lack paired references. No results establish production readiness or insurer deployment. Dashboard screenshots should be included only after actual live capture; upload-dependent sections require actual app state.''')
put(groups[6],'Dashboard_Evidence_Status.md','''# Dashboard Evidence Status\n\nThe Streamlit app was launched locally at `http://localhost:8502`. The Overview page rendered and displayed the expected empty-state prompt because no document was loaded in this fresh app session. The sidebar exposed the eight sections: Overview, Document Upload, Image Processing Lab, OCR Results, Document Intelligence, Claim Validation, AI Summary, and Evaluation.\n\nA file-backed screenshot set was not saved. The computer-use browser returned an in-session Overview image, but no workspace file-save mechanism was available. The remaining seven views were not independently captured/verified in this audit. Upload-dependent views require a processed document. This package contains no dashboard screenshots and must not be represented as having them.''')
put(groups[7],'References.md','''# References\n\n[1] N. Otsu, “A Threshold Selection Method from Gray-Level Histograms,” IEEE Trans. Syst., Man, Cybern., vol. 9, no. 1, pp. 62–66, 1979, doi: 10.1109/TSMC.1979.4310076. https://doi.org/10.1109/TSMC.1979.4310076\n[2] K. Zuiderveld, “Contrast Limited Adaptive Histogram Equalization,” in Graphics Gems IV, 1994, pp. 474–485.\n[3] R. Smith, “An Overview of the Tesseract OCR Engine,” Proc. ICDAR, 2007, pp. 629–633, doi: 10.1109/ICDAR.2007.4376991.\n[4] G. Jaume et al., “FUNSD: A Dataset for Form Understanding in Noisy Scanned Documents,” Proc. ICDAR Workshops, 2019.\n\nCheck source dataset redistribution terms before sharing data.''')

